# backend/Dockerfile.dockerignore — what `docker build` may see of the repo when it builds backend/Dockerfile (OPS-1).
#
# Docker reads an ignore file named after the Dockerfile, next to it, instead of the root .dockerignore.
# So this list belongs to this one image; OPS-5's `web` image can have its own. Paths are relative to the
# build context, the repo root, as in the Dockerfile's COPY lines.
#
# An allowlist at every level: everything is shut out, then only what the build uses is let back in, by
# name. A local build sends untracked files too, not only what git tracks: a .env with secrets, a sheet
# with real shop data, an editor's settings. They all stay out unless someone names them here on purpose
# (03 §10, ADR-0028). An image gets pushed to a registry (OPS-2): whatever goes in can be pulled out again.

# Shut out everything...
*

# ...then let in what the Python stages copy: the code and the package lists.
!backend/apps
!backend/config
!backend/manage.py
!backend/pyproject.toml
!backend/uv.lock
!backend/.python-version

# ...and what `pnpm build` reads: the sources, the texts, the public files, the package lists and the
# build settings. Lint, format and test settings stay out: the build doesn't use them.
!frontend/src
!frontend/messages
!frontend/public
!frontend/package.json
!frontend/pnpm-lock.yaml
!frontend/pnpm-workspace.yaml
!frontend/next.config.ts
!frontend/tsconfig.json
!frontend/postcss.config.mjs

# Inside the folders let in above, shut out again what Python leaves behind, and any .env.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.env
**/.env.*
